---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { freshness } from "../lib/exports/load";
import { INDICATORS } from "../lib/indicators";
import { href } from "../lib/site";

const tables = freshness();
---

<Layout title="Methodology">
  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This site shows evidence about Missouri officials, not verdicts. It has no composite "bias score": any
    comparison between officials ranks one measured number, shown with its source, and every official gets the
    same layout whatever their party. Each figure links to the public record it came from.
  </p>

  <section class="card" id="indicators">
    <h2>Ideology indicators</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Each indicator is shown on its own scale. Scales use a neutral violet-to-orange palette; party is shown
      only as a text label.
    </p>
    {Object.entries(INDICATORS).map(([key, m]) => (
      <div class="ind">
        <h3>
          {m.label} <code class="small muted">{key}</code>
        </h3>
        <p>{m.meaning}</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Scale:</strong>{" "}
          {m.low === "%"
            ? `${m.domain[0]}% to ${m.domain[1]}%`
            : `${m.domain[0]} (${m.low}) to ${m.domain[1]} (${m.high})`}
          . <strong>Limits:</strong> {m.limits}
        </p>
        <p class="small muted">Source: {m.source}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="matching">
    <h2>How records are matched to officials</h2>
    <p>
      Many sources (campaign finance, lobbyist reports) identify people by name only. Each matched record
      keeps a <code>match_confidence</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>exact id</strong>: joined on a shared identifier (FEC candidate id, bioguide id).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>exact (MEC crosswalk)</strong>: the Missouri Ethics Commission's own candidate-committee list
        names the committee.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>high</strong>: first and last name both match.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>medium</strong>: surname matches but the first-name signal is weaker (only the first three
        letters, or a surname-first committee name with no other official sharing the surname). These carry a
        <span class="badge medium">possible match</span> badge, and "Hide possible matches" on any profile
        removes them from every table and chart.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="committee-donors">
    <h2>Following committee money one step back</h2>
    <p>
      When a Missouri committee gives to an official, the Missouri Ethics Commission records the giver by name
      only, with no committee ID. To show who funds that committee, its name is matched to the names Missouri
      committees file under, trying in order: a short list of known alternate names (acronyms such as HRCC or
      AMECPAC), each group checked against the committee's filings or its own filed name; a committee ID
      written into the name; the same name, ignoring case, with punctuation read as a space and runs of spaces
      collapsed ("Health PAC" and "HealthPAC" still differ); the name with common abbreviations spelled out
      ("MO" as Missouri, "Assn" as Association); and the name without words like PAC, Political Action
      Committee or Fund (or a short name the filer put in parentheses). A committee only counts if it was
      active within a year of the gift, and only when exactly one committee fits. When several do, no link is
      made.
    </p>
    <p>
      About 71% of the committee-to-committee dollars on record match one Missouri committee this way. Most of
      the rest come from federal and out-of-state PACs, which report to the FEC or another state instead, and
      from less common groups known by an acronym that no name rule can expand. Because the match rests on a
      name, every link carries a <span class="badge medium">possible match</span> badge.
    </p>
    <p>
      Above the trails, each profile adds up who gave to the committees listed there, by where the trail goes
      out of view. It counts only those committees: for each, its 10 largest donors that are not individuals
      plus its individual donors added together, across every year on file. This is money those committees
      took in, not money given to the official; the profile states how much those committees gave the
      official. The listed committees sometimes give to each other, so some dollars appear twice.
    </p>
    <p>
      The trail ends at businesses and other organizations, nonprofits included: the Ethics Commission gets
      donor reports from committees, not from the organizations that give to them, so where an organization's
      own money came from isn't in these records. Individuals are summed into one row rather than listed by
      name.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="coverage">
    <h2>Coverage and gaps</h2>
    <p>Each profile opens with a coverage strip. The states mean different things:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>● available</strong>: records were found and are shown.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>○ none found</strong>: we looked in a source that covers this office and found nothing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>⊘ not public</strong>: the records exist but aren't published online (for example, Missouri
        personal financial disclosures need a Sunshine Law request). We can't see them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>– n/a</strong>: the measure doesn't apply to this office (for example, judges don't cast
        roll-call votes).
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Under the strip, one sentence lists every item marked "not public" or "none found". It uses the same
      wording for every official and party, and it is left out when nothing is missing. Items marked n/a are
      not listed. The sentence also names items the strip leaves out as cells, such as financial disclosures
      for state officials, so a gap there is still stated.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What you can conclude from "none found":</strong> our sources, as of the last update, held no
      matching record. That is weaker than "there is nothing". A source may not cover this office or this
      court (CourtListener, for example, has no Missouri circuit-court cases, the state trial courts), a name
      may not have matched, or a filing may be late. Do not read it as a clean record or as a sign of
      wrongdoing.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What you can conclude from "none recorded":</strong> no public list of the item exists, so we
      hold only what we found and added by hand. It does not mean the official has nothing to report.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What you can conclude from "not public":</strong> nothing about the official. The records may
      exist and may say anything; we simply can't see them without a records request. A blank here is a hole
      in the public record, not a verdict, and it says nothing either way.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="courts">
    <h2>Court records</h2>
    <p>
      Cases come from CourtListener: published opinions and dockets that name the official as the judge on the
      panel or as an attorney. CourtListener covers appellate and federal courts well but holds no Missouri
      circuit-court (state trial court) cases, so a circuit judge with few cases here may simply sit in a
      court it doesn't index.
    </p>
    <p>
      Profiles count cases per year and list the most recent ones with the court's own disposition text. They
      do not tally outcomes (affirmed, reversed, who won): turning dispositions into win/loss figures needs a
      reviewed method, and raw rates mislead when judges hear different kinds of cases.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="sponsorship">
    <h2>Bills sponsored in the House</h2>
    <p>
      For each current House member and House session, we count the House bills (HB) and House joint
      resolutions (HJR) they are the primary sponsor of, as the House's own bill actions list them.
      Co-sponsors, handlers and conferees are not counted, and neither are resolutions (HR, HCR), which are
      adopted on a voice vote or not at all, or Senate bills. Appropriation bills are included: the Budget
      chair sponsors them and nearly all pass, so that member's share is high for that reason.
    </p>
    <p>
      A bill is <em>decided</em> unless it is still pending (the session is open) or awaiting the governor.
      <em>Passed the House</em> counts decided bills the House passed on third reading, including ones that
      then died in the Senate or were vetoed. <em>Became law</em> counts an enacted HB (signed, line-item
      vetoed, veto overridden or filed without signature) or an HJR sent to the voters; a truly agreed bill
      with no governor action recorded is decided but not counted as law. A percent is shown only when at
      least 5 bills were decided; it is the count over the decided bills, not a score.
    </p>
    <p>
      Only current officials have profiles, so bills sponsored by members who have since left office are not
      counted. That makes the earliest sessions (2017 and 2018) a survivor group, mostly long-serving leaders,
      and their numbers are not comparable with recent sessions.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="vote-stage">
    <h2>Stage of a Missouri vote</h2>
    <p>
      Wherever the profile lists individual Missouri roll calls, each roll call is labeled with the kind of
      question the vote decided, so a final-passage vote is not mistaken for an amendment, a procedural motion
      or a consent-calendar bill. It is a label only: attendance, party unity and the awards do not read it.
      Federal votes keep their own description and get no stage.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Perfection</strong>: the House or Senate working over a bill's text, usually by amendment,
        before it is read a third time. <strong>Third reading</strong>: the vote to pass the bill in that
        chamber.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Consent calendar, third reading</strong>: a bill on the House consent or federal-mandate
        calendar. These bills are grouped and pass with little or no floor debate.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Informal calendar</strong>, added in brackets: the House calendar item was marked informal,
        which means the bill was listed on the informal calendar when the vote was taken. The Senate journal
        has no such marker, so Senate votes are never labeled this way.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Senate amendments</strong> and <strong>Conference report</strong>: the vote on the other
        chamber's changes to a bill, or on the conference version. In the House these stages also include the
        final-passage vote that follows (the bill truly agreed to and finally passed), so one measure can show
        two roll calls under the same stage.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Veto override</strong>, <strong>Amendment</strong> and <strong>Emergency clause</strong>:
        votes on those questions, not the vote to pass the bill in that chamber.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Procedural motion</strong>, <strong>Previous question</strong> and{" "}
        <strong>Quorum call</strong>: votes about how the chamber runs (approving the journal, suspending the
        rules, reconsidering, adjourning, ending debate, checking attendance), not about a bill's contents.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Resolution</strong> and <strong>Other question</strong>: a resolution, or a vote we recognise
        but that fits none of the stages above (for example an officer election). Some Other question votes
        are still being sorted into proper stages (for example House roll calls on bills carrying request
        messages, tracked in issue 816).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Stage not identified</strong>: the stage is worked out from the House calendar item or the
        wording of the Senate journal, and when neither says enough (some House votes on the General calendar,
        and some Senate journal lines), we say so rather than guess.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="attendance">
    <h2>Attendance</h2>
    <p>
      Attendance is the share of roll-call votes on which the official was listed, per chamber and session,
      where they voted yea, nay or present. Every other recorded position counts as not attended. The profile
      chart's "Not attended" view shows the other side of the same figure, as a share of recorded votes: one
      bar per session, made of three stacked parts:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Absent or not voting</strong>: marked absent (MO House and Senate) or not voting (Congress).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Absent with leave</strong>: an excused absence, as recorded in the MO Senate journals.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Recused</strong>: the MO Senate's "Excused from voting", a Senate Rule 91 recusal by a member
        who is on the floor. It is not an absence, but it is not counted as attended either, so it lowers the
        attendance figure.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Only the MO Senate records leave and recusals. The MO House and Congress sources don't, so a 0 in those
      bars (or a dash in the table) does not mean the official was never excused: an excused House absence
      shows up as plain absent. The chamber average tick is 100 minus the whole chamber's attendance for that
      session (every member who voted that session, not only the officials this site covers). It lumps all
      non-attended positions together, because the export doesn't split out the chamber's excused absences or
      recusals, so compare it with the end of the official's whole bar, not with one part alone.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="per-diem-mileage">
    <h2>Per diem and mileage</h2>
    <p>
      State legislators' profiles show the per diem (legislative daily expense allowance) and in-state mileage
      the state paid them, from the Missouri Accountability Portal's "State Expenditures" datasets on
      data.mo.gov (agency LEGISLATURE).
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Years</strong> are state fiscal years, July to June: FY2026 is July 2025 to June 2026.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Amounts</strong> are what the state paid, not what was claimed, and each is one payee's total
        for the fiscal year and line, not one payment. Per diem is claimed for days attended and mileage
        follows the distance from the district to the Capitol, so neither measures performance; mileage is
        shown as context only.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Matching</strong>: the state lists legal names ("ATCHISON, FRANKLIN DARRELL"), so a payee is
        matched to an official through the state payroll, which prints the same names. A payee whose name fits
        more than one legislator is left out, and a looser fit (a nickname fit, or a surname shared with
        another legislator) is flagged "possible match". Only people who hold an office on this site today are
        matched, so a year can be missing because the payee was not matched, not because nothing was paid.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="pay-per-vote">
    <h2>Pay per vote attended</h2>
    <p>
      Under the attendance chart, each official's pay is divided by the votes they attended, for each calendar
      year and for the years together. Both halves are counted over the same calendar year, using the date of
      each vote, so a year's regular and extraordinary sessions are one row and a Congress is split across its
      two years.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Votes attended</strong>: recorded votes where the official voted yea, nay or present, the same
        count attendance is based on. Absences, "not voting" and Senate Rule 91 recusals are not attended, so
        a recusal does not count here (whether it should is an open question).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pay</strong>, for Missouri officials: gross pay from the state payroll (data.mo.gov, 2017
        onward), meaning everything the payroll shows as paid in that calendar year, not the base salary.
        Several payroll rows in a year, such as a member and a leadership title, are added together. Names on
        the payroll are matched to officials, and unmatched or ambiguous rows are left out. A year whose pay
        rests on a name-only match is flagged "possible match", and hiding possible matches also drops it from
        the total. For members of Congress it is the statutory annual salary of the office, because federal
        pay is not published per person; the year still in progress is left out until it ends, and a member
        who joined mid-year is shown with the full annual salary.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Totals</strong>: the sum of pay over the years that have both pay and votes, divided by the
        votes attended in those same years. A year with votes but no pay counted (all years before 2017, a
        year the payroll has not published yet, a federal year still in progress, or an official whose payroll
        rows could not be matched) is shown but left out of the total, and the note under the table says how
        many years that left out.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A dash</strong> means there is no figure: no pay counted, or no attended vote that year.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The figure divides a whole year's pay by roll-call votes only. It is not an hourly rate or a price per
      day of work: members also do committee work and constituent work that no vote records, and a chamber
      with more roll calls in a year gives a smaller number. A Missouri legislator who started or left
      mid-year has only the pay actually received, so a partial year can look high. For Congress a partial
      year counts the full annual salary against only the votes cast, which also inflates the figure. Compare
      it between officials and years with that in mind.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="awards">
    <h2>Awards</h2>
    <p>
      Each award ranks one measured number, among officials holding the same office, and, except for bills
      passed, comes with its opposite (lowest and highest attendance, and so on). Ranks use the unrounded
      figure, so two officials shown at the same rounded percentage can hold different ranks; only exact ties
      share a rank (this holds for every award, including the lowest share of money from PACs, committees and
      parties). The top five qualify unless an award sets its own cutoff (the awards_threshold setting). For
      the attendance and party-unity pairs, which rank one number over one field, each end's cutoff is at most
      half the field (ties at the cutoff can add more), and an official tied into both lists is left out of
      both, so nobody holds both ends of a pair in one period. An office needs at least five qualifying
      officials in a period, so single-seat offices like governor have no awards. An official who qualified
      for the same award in consecutive years gets a marker such as X3 (3 qualifying years in a row; for the
      federal money share, 3 qualifying cycles in a row). Years the official wasn't ranked for that award (in
      another office, not eligible for the measure, or too few officials to rank) are skipped: they neither
      add to nor break the run, so the years in a run can span more calendar years than the number shown. A
      year ranked outside the cutoff breaks it. Lifetime rankings have no marker.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Attendance</strong>: share of recorded votes where the official voted yea, nay or present; at
        least 100 recorded votes in the period.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Money from PACs, committees and parties</strong>: dollars received from them, and (the
        opposite) the lowest share of all money raised, among officials raising at least $10,000. The Missouri
        Ethics Commission files PACs as committees, so the two are counted together. Name-only matches are
        left out. For members of Congress the share is per two-year FEC cycle (shown as, for example, "the
        2025–26 cycle"; the period picker lists it under 2026), so it has no odd-year rows. Its denominator is
        the FEC's total receipts for the cycle, which include loans and transfers as well as contributions (a
        known limitation), and a cycle with no FEC total gets no share. The most-money award for federal
        officials still counts calendar years.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Party loyalty</strong>: party unity (the share of party-line votes cast with the official's
        own party) in the official's current chamber, per calendar year and over all loaded votes, with the
        number of party-line votes it rests on shown beside it. Needs at least 20 party-line votes in the
        period (the awards_min_party_line_votes setting, default 20). Maverick needs at least one vote against
        the party. The lifetime figure is the same number the profile page shows; yearly figures appear with
        the award only.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Bills passed</strong>: the share of House bills (HB) and House joint resolutions (HJR) the
        member sponsored as primary sponsor, in sessions that have ended, that the House passed, out of those
        whose outcome is known (pending bills are not counted). A year adds its regular and special sessions;
        lifetime adds the counts. Needs at least 10 decided bills (the awards_min_bills_decided setting;
        provisional, see issue 773) and at least one bill passed. There is no lowest-share list, because most
        members pass none.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="interest-votes">
    <h2>Interests and votes</h2>
    <p>
      Where the site shows money or gifts from an interest next to votes on related bills, that is{" "}
      <strong>co-occurrence, not causation</strong>. A donation alongside a vote doesn't show the vote was
      bought, and officials often get money from groups that already agree with them.
    </p>
    <p>
      How the match is made: donors and lobbyist principals are assigned to an industry by name keywords, and
      each industry is tied to bill subject terms from the House subject index and Senate topics. A vote
      appears when the bill carries one of those terms and the industry gave the official money in or before
      that year. Money shown is the total through the latest such vote. Keyword matching misses donors whose
      names don't say what they do, and many large PACs (leadership or pass-through PACs) belong to no single
      industry. <a href={href("/pacs/")}>How PACs move money</a> explains how money can pass through PACs and
      nonprofits before it reaches a candidate.
    </p>
    <p>
      The example list shows one vote per bill, newest bills first. When a bill had several roll calls on its
      latest day, the one shown is picked in this order: final passage, then any other question, then an
      amendment; otherwise the day's last roll call. A motion to reconsider is never taken as passage. Senate
      Rule 91 recusals (a senator on the floor excusing themselves from a vote) are always listed (one per
      bill), even past the newest bills, because they are the votes most likely to matter here.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="district-businesses">
    <h2>Businesses in their area</h2>
    <p>
      The businesses come from EPA's Facility Registry Service: every site EPA or a state environmental
      program regulates, with its address, coordinates, industry code and owner. Each site is placed in the
      state House and Senate district, U.S. House district and city that contain it. It is the only free,
      named and geocoded list of Missouri businesses we found, but it covers regulated sites only (factories,
      fuel stations, utilities, repair shops, farms with permits), so offices, stores and most service
      businesses are missing. About a third of the sites have no coordinates and so no district. The industry
      is the site's NAICS code or, when it has none, the NAICS sector its older SIC code maps to; about 4 in
      10 sites have neither.
    </p>
    <p>
      A site is tied to a donor when a non-individual contributor's name equals the site's, its owner's or its
      parent company's, ignoring case, with punctuation read as a space, a leading "the" and words like Inc,
      LLC and Corp dropped, or when both names carry a brand from a short hand-checked list of large companies
      (so "Ameren Missouri PAC" ties to a "UNION ELECTRIC CO" substation). A company that gives under another
      name is missed, and the amount shown is everything those donors gave the official, not money tied to one
      site. A company giving to the official for the area it operates in is{" "}
      <strong>co-occurrence, not influence</strong>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="freshness">
    <h2>Sources and data freshness</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Dataset</th>
            <th>Exported</th>
            <th>Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {tables.map((t) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <code>{t.table}</code>
              </td>
              <td>{t.exported_at?.slice(0, 10) ?? "—"}</td>
              <td>
                {t.sample ? <span class="badge medium">sample data (model not built yet)</span> : "real"}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small muted">
      Sources include the Missouri Ethics Commission, Missouri House and Senate journals, Open States, the
      FEC, Voteview, Shor-McCarty, U.S. House and Senate financial disclosures, CourtListener and the Federal
      Judicial Center. The repository's docs/sources.md has the per-source details and known quirks.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  h1 {
    margin-top: 24px;
  }
  /* all prose: keep each card as wide as its text (75ch plus padding) so wide screens don't leave an empty
     right half inside every card */
  .card {
    max-width: calc(75ch + 42px);
  }
  .ind + .ind {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }
</style>
